        "summary":  "David Linthicum examines the tradeoffs of serverless AI architecture across Amazon Bedrock, Azure OpenAI Service, and Google Cloud Vertex AI, arguing that serverless is not universally optimal despite its growing default status in enterprise AI deployments. Per-token and per-request pricing models work well for variable, unpredictable workloads but can be significantly more expensive than provisioned infrastructure for steady-state inference at scale. The article advises matching infrastructure model to actual traffic patterns rather than defaulting to serverless for all AI use cases.",
        "execSummary":  "The serverless AI default is becoming a costly assumption for enterprises with predictable, high-volume inference workloads — the per-token billing model that benefits bursty applications penalizes steady-state production deployments at scale. For enterprise cloud and AI platform leaders, this is a call to audit current AI workloads against actual traffic profiles and make deliberate provisioning decisions rather than accepting serverless as the path of least resistance. The practical implication is a workload segmentation discipline: volatile workloads on serverless, predictable workloads on reserved capacity, with cost attribution rigorous enough to distinguish between them. Organizations that establish this governance now will avoid significant waste as enterprise AI inference volumes grow.",
        "vendorLean":  "Neutral",
        "imageUrl":  "https://www.infoworld.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/4218919-0-34215100-1788858096-shutterstock_1277815804.jpg?quality=50\u0026strip=all\u0026w=1024",
        "isNew":  false
